Louise was just 13 when she became a  victim of online grooming now five years  later she's waived her right to  anonymity to work with the charity safe  and sound and warn others of the dangers  of social media what they do to you they  mentally wreck you you realize in the  end you can't trust anyone  it really did ruin me I thought if I  hadn't have moved on the hand of worked  with these guys I like say what probably  would have killed myself  Louise shares the concerns of the NSPCC  that Facebook's plans to offer  end-to-end encryption on its apps could  allow sex offenders like the ones who  targeted her to go undetected the NSPCC  found a total of nine thousand two  hundred and fifty nine instances  recorded by police where the method of  communication was known almost twenty  two percent were recorded on Instagram  and 19 percent on Facebook only three  percent were recorded on whatsapp which  is already encrypted nearly 57 percent  of the offenses were recorded on other  platforms what's so disturbing about  Facebook's proposals is that really this  means that Facebook sites will become a  one-stop grooming shop the algorithm  will recommend children for groomers to  be able to contact once a groomer has  made that initial contact we know that  then in just a few clicks they'll be  able to start sending encrypted messages  which neither Facebook nor law  enforcement will be able to access  Facebook says it already has systems in  place to help keep young people safe in  a statement the company says there is no  place for grooming or child exploitation  on our platforms we use technology to  proactively remove it and a developing  further ways to detect patterns of  harmful behavior in order to ban and  report those responsible  Lois says she only survived her ordeal  thanks to safe and sound now she's  urging all young people and their  families to use the strongest possible  privacy settings on their social media  and not to be afraid to seek help if  they need to  Fraser Maud Sky News Darby
 

